  "rules": {
    "display_mechanism_scope_v1": "The LCD reference says liquid crystals do not emit light directly and instead modulate light from a backlight or reflector through polarizers. The OLED reference says an organic electroluminescent layer emits light in response to electric current and that AMOLED uses a TFT backplane to switch individual pixels. These are mechanism differences, not proof that every OLED phone is brighter, sharper, more comfortable, more efficient, or better outdoors than every LCD phone. A technology label must not be promoted into a universal quality ranking.",
    "display_measurement_context_v1": "Refresh rate is how often display hardware updates its buffer and is distinct from touch response rate and the frame rate generated by content. Luminance is an objective photometric quantity in candela per square metre or nits, while brightness is the subjective impression. Contrast ratio compares brightest white with darkest black, but the reference says manufacturer figures are not necessarily comparable because measurement methods vary and real-room light lowers seen contrast. The Retina reference says there is no fixed minimum pixel density across devices because pixel visibility depends on viewing distance and angular resolution. Therefore scrolling smoothness, outdoor visibility, dark-scene contrast, text sharpness, and comfort are separate properties and conditions rather than one quality number.",
    "listing_level_display_matrix_v1": "The frozen seller pages advertise: a 179.99-dollar TCL 10L with a 6.53-inch FHD+ LCD; a 134.99-dollar Verizon-only Samsung A51 with a 6.5-inch Super AMOLED display; a 360-dollar international Motorola Edge 20 Lite with a 6.7-inch FHD+ 90 Hz OLED; and a 1496-dollar Xperia bundle listing with a 6.5-inch 4K HDR OLED at 120 Hz. These pages establish seller wording, exact configuration caveats, and snapshot prices. They do not independently measure luminance, reflected-light contrast, color accuracy, motion clarity, power use, eye comfort, or the portion of the full-phone price caused by the screen. The lower-priced AMOLED listing and the widely separated configurations also show why price and panel label cannot be treated as a monotonic display-quality scale.",
    "scoped_display_experience_v1": "Interpret the 120 Hz discussion against the definition that refresh rate is distinct from touch response and content frame rate, and interpret the brightness comparison against the objective-luminance, subjective-brightness, and ambient-contrast boundaries. Users still disagree: some call 120 Hz important or immediately noticeable, while others say it became nonessential on a phone, that 60 Hz did not become unusable, or that the savings can matter; the original poster mainly calls, texts, browses, and watches YouTube. In another thread, an XR user reports eye strain after only five to ten minutes with an iPhone 14 store unit, and replies describe different personal outcomes and possible explanations without controlled diagnosis. A third user reports that an older LCD looked brighter or better in some shots, while replies suggest settings or a defect. These are scoped experiences and unresolved comparisons, not prevalence, controlled causation, or proof about the four frozen listings.",
    "person_specific_display_trial_v1": "Define separate pass conditions before shopping: readable text at the buyer's normal distance and font size; acceptable scrolling at the chosen refresh setting; acceptable video with the buyer's actual content; readable maps, messages, and controls in the buyer's outdoor conditions; tolerable brightness, dimming, color, and visual comfort in a sustained session; and acceptable battery, network, storage, warranty, operating-system, and total-phone tradeoffs. Use comparable settings and content, test each exact phone rather than a panel label, and record whether each task changes enough to matter. Listing claims can form a shortlist but cannot replace this test.",
    "evidence_bounded_display_value_decision_v1": "There is no supported universal middle tier, price breakpoint, or bragging-rights threshold. Choose the least expensive returnable exact configuration that passes the buyer's defined reading, messaging, scrolling, video, outdoor-visibility, comfort, and whole-phone requirements. Stop paying for additional display specifications when controlled side-by-side or normal-use testing shows no meaningful improvement in those tasks for this buyer, while preserving any tradeoffs that do change. If comparable measurements, exact configuration details, return terms, or hands-on results are missing, state the uncertainty and defer rather than assigning an invented stopping price."
  },
